# Line endings, pinned.
#
# Every text file in this repository is LF in the index and LF in the working
# tree, on every platform. That has to be *stated*, because Git for Windows
# installs with core.autocrlf=true — windows-latest does not override the
# installer default, and actions/checkout never touches it — which smudges an
# LF-committed file to CRLF on the way out. gofmt parses CRLF without complaint
# and prints LF, so it calls every such file unformatted: the Windows lint job
# reported exactly that, 144 findings across the terminal adapter, each pointing
# at line 1, none of them about the code.
#
# The mechanism is gitattributes(5), and both halves are load-bearing:
#
#   "If the text attribute is unspecified, Git uses the core.autocrlf
#    configuration variable to determine if the file should be converted."
#     -> setting text explicitly is what takes core.autocrlf out of the answer.
#   "eol=lf — This setting uses the same line endings in the working directory
#    as in the index when the file is checked out."
#     -> text=auto alone would still normalize on commit and hand a Windows
#        checkout CRLF, because its own eol default on Windows is crlf.
#
# internal/checkout/line_endings_test.go holds this file to it: it asks git what
# text and eol resolve to for every tracked path, and what the checkout actually
# looks like, so a rule that silently stops applying fails a test rather than
# reappearing as a wall of gofmt findings.

# The catch-all, so that a file type nobody thought about is LF by default.
# Inferred from content, which is the right bet for prose and the wrong bet for
# anything executable or byte-exact — those are named below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The extensions this repository is made of, typed as text by name. Same
# behaviour today; the point is that the outcome no longer depends on a
# heuristic noticing that, say, a .sh file is text. Shell scripts and Make
# recipes are the sharp edge: with a CR they do not merely look unformatted,
# they fail to run or to build.
*.go     text eol=lf
*.conf   text eol=lf
*.gitignore text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.mod    text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.sum    text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.tape   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
LICENSE  text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Binary by name, never by detection: adapter-guide/tlv-samples/*.bin are byte
# exact TLV recordings the adapter tests replay, and a fixture that happens to
# read as text would otherwise be "corrected" into a different file. The -eol
# backs out of the catch-all's eol=lf: on a path git is not converting at all,
# an eol answer that says lf would be a lie to anyone reading `git check-attr`.
*.bin  binary -eol
*.gif  binary -eol
*.jpg  binary -eol
*.pdf  binary -eol
*.png  binary -eol
*.wav  binary -eol
*.webp binary -eol
